# Key References  

- Zhai et al., *“Generative LLMs for Knowledge Representation”*【32†L73-L81】【32†L82-L90】 – A 2025 review showing that modern LLMs can automate concept map generation, capturing semantic relationships in text. It argues LLMs “capture semantic connections across domains” and “articulate them in semantically coherent ways”【32†L82-L90】, motivating our use of GPT to spawn and connect ideas.  
- Liu et al., *“XISM: an eXploratory Interactive Semantic Map Tool”*【25†L46-L54】 – Describes an interactive tool that generates candidate semantic maps and lets users iteratively refine edges. Highlights combining automated graph construction with human (or UI) refinement. We draw on this for Beefythiq’s **interactive UX**: start with data-driven graph generation and allow iterative exploration, echoing their human-in-the-loop philosophy【25†L46-L54】.  
- Leviathan et al., *“Generative UI”* (Google Research, 2025)【13†L13-L21】 – Introduces the concept of AI-generated interfaces. They note that “LLMs generate not just content but the entire interface” and that custom UIs can be created on the fly for any prompt【13†L13-L21】. While Beefythiq has a fixed UI shell, this inspires the idea that our UI *grows dynamically* with the data, not rigidly charted – i.e. the visual “world” adapts to the generated concepts.  
- Pregasen, *“Vector DB vs. Knowledge Graphs for RAG”*【23†L151-L156】【19†L3-L11】 – Industry analysis comparing vector embeddings to graph databases. We cite their summary that vector DBs excel at semantic search (converting text to embeddings【23†L87-L95】) but lack explicit relationships, whereas knowledge graphs explicitly link entities【23†L151-L156】. We follow their recommendation to use a hybrid approach【19†L3-L11】 to leverage both fast semantic retrieval and structured reasoning.  
- Fauconnier & Turner (conceptual blending theory)【7†L130-L135】 – Not a new paper but the foundational idea that novel concepts arise by blending elements from different “input spaces”. Beefythiq’s recombination is essentially implementing conceptual blending. We cite the theory definition that “elements from diverse scenarios are blended… in a subconscious process” to contextualize why our mixes (e.g. *MASS+PRESENCE → SUBSTANTIAL*) can yield meaningful new concepts【7†L130-L135】.  
- Lin et al. (2024), *“Node Importance Estimation with LLMs”*【34†L86-L94】 – Recent work on ranking graph nodes. They note early methods like PageRank measure node significance based on connections. This parallels Beefythiq’s **girth metric**: essentially a node’s importance in the concept graph. We draw on that by treating girth like a centrality score and promoting nodes akin to high-ranked pages【34†L86-L94】.  
- [Additional UX/AI sources] “Concept Maps in UX” (IxDF, 2026) – general background on concept maps in design; AI-driven mind-map generation (Chang et al., 2025); [*these can be added if needed for completeness*].  

Each cited source informed our design: whether it’s the idea that AI can build knowledge structures【32†L73-L81】【32†L82-L90】, that interactive graph tools aid understanding【25†L46-L54】, or how best to combine vector embeddings with graph data【23†L151-L156】【19†L3-L11】.  Together, they validate Beefythiq’s approach: an **LLM-powered, growing concept graph** with a responsive UI and schema for tracking concept “girth.”